### Goal Model Design

The goal model is structured to capture the mission's requirements, decomposing the main objective into sub-goals and tasks, considering parallel execution where beneficial.

#### Goals Table

| Name       | Text                                                                 | Runtime  | Goal Type   | Relation | Justification                                                                 |
|------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|-------------|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| G1         | Collect and Deliver Resources                                         | -         | Achieve     | -         | Ensures mission success by completing both collection and delivery.          |
| G2         | Collection Phase                                                     | -         | Perform     | AND       | Both selecting order and requesting resources are necessary for collection.  |
| G3         | Delivery Phase                                                      | -         | Perform     | AND       | Both delivering and handling low battery are crucial for delivery.           |
| G2.1       | Select Order of Storage Visits                                       | -         | Perform     | AND       | Necessary to optimize the path and waiting time for efficient collection.    |
| G2.2       | Request and Retrieve Resources                                       | -         | Perform     | AND       | Essential step after reaching a storage location to collect resources.        |
| G3.1       | Deliver Resources to Specified Location                             | -         | Perform     | AND       | Required to complete the delivery phase successfully.                       |
| G3.2       | Handle Low Battery in Delivery Phase                                | -         | Perform     | AND       | Ensures mission continuity if battery drops, by transferring resources.        |
| G3.3       | Trigger Alert and Report if Resources Cannot Be Returned           | -         | Perform     | AND       | Critical for accountability and system integrity if resources are lost.       |

#### Tasks Table

| Name       | Text                                                                 | Relation | Location          | Number of Robots | Justification                                                                 |
|------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| AT1        | Collect Resources from Storage                                       | AND       | Storage Locations | [1, n]           | Multiple robots can collect from different storages in parallel.              |
| AT2        | Deliver Resources to Specified Location                             | AND       | Delivery Location | [1, n]           | Multiple robots can deliver to the same location in parallel for efficiency.   |

### Logical Relationships

- **G1 -> G2, G3 [AND]**  
  Justification: Both collection and delivery are essential for mission success.

- **G2 -> G2.1, G2.2 [AND]**  
  Justification: Both steps are necessary for the collection phase.

- **G3 -> G3.1, G3.2, G3.3 [AND]**  
  Justification: All steps are crucial for the delivery phase.

- **G2.1 -> AT1 [AND]**  
  Justification: Selecting the order is followed by collecting resources.

- **G2.2 -> AT1 [AND]**  
  Justification: Requesting resources follows collection.

- **G3.1 -> AT2 [AND]**  
  Justification: Delivering resources is the final step in delivery.

- **G3.2 -> AT2 [AND]**  
  Justification: Handling low battery ensures resources are transferred before recharge.

This model ensures efficient resource management and mission continuity through parallel execution of tasks where possible, while maintaining necessary sequential steps for critical operations.
